{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.OrbitCount","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.OrbitCount","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.GroupTheory.Perm.Cycle.Type","lean:module:Mathlib.GroupTheory.Perm.Support","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":18,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect_swap_mul_of_not_sameCycle","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect_swap_mul_of_not_sameCycle","kind":"theorem","line":587,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrbitCount.lean#L587-L600","doc":"**The merge case, DERIVED from (S) and (R).** When `a` and `b` lie in different\ncycles of `σ`, those two orbits merge and the defect rises by exactly one.\n\n`swap a b` is an involution, so `swap a b * σ` has `a` and `b` in one cycle by (R),\nand applying (S) to it returns `σ`. Nothing new is proved here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect_swap_mul_of_sameCycle","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect_swap_mul_of_sameCycle","kind":"theorem","line":562,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrbitCount.lean#L562-L585","doc":"**(S)** Within one cycle, the transposition splits it and the defect drops by\nexactly one. **Proved.**\n\n`a` and `b` lie on one cycle, so `b = σ^k a` for a least `k ≥ 1`; the arc induction\n`orbitDefect_swap_mul_aux` walks that arc down to the adjacent case `k = 1`, which is\n`orbitDefect_swap_apply_mul`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect_swap_apply_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect_swap_apply_mul","kind":"theorem","line":427,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrbitCount.lean#L427-L469","doc":"**(S-adj)** Multiplying by the transposition of `x` with its own image `f x` removes\n`x` from its cycle, dropping the defect by exactly one.\n\nReduces to the single-cycle case by splitting `f = cycleOf f x * (f * (cycleOf f x)⁻¹)`\ninto disjoint halves (`disjoint_mul_inv_of_mem_cycleFactorsFinset`) — the transposition\ntouches only the first — and applying `orbitDefect_mul_of_disjoint`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.sameCycle_swap_mul_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.sameCycle_swap_mul_iff","kind":"theorem","line":385,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrbitCount.lean#L385-L391","doc":"**(R) and (R2) together: a transposition exactly toggles whether the two points\nshare a cycle.** The complete dynamical picture at `{a, b}`, and all the dynamical\ninput a counting proof of (S) needs — what remains there is the counting itself. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.not_sameCycle_swap_mul_of_sameCycle","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.not_sameCycle_swap_mul_of_sameCycle","kind":"theorem","line":323,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrbitCount.lean#L323-L383","doc":"**(R2)** The converse of (R), and the *separation* half of the split: if `a` and\n`b` already share a cycle, the transposition puts them in **different** cycles of the\nproduct. **Proved**, by the same walk.\n\nTake `k` least positive with `σ^k a ∈ {a, b}`. If `σ^k a = a` then `b` never appears\non `a`'s orbit, contradicting the hypothesis; so `σ^k a = b`, and then `τ = swap a b * σ`\nfollows `σ` up to step `k−1` and closes back to `a` at step `k` — `τ^k a = a`, with the\nwhole `τ`-orbit of `a` sitting inside `{σ^j a : j < k}`, which avoids `b`.\n\nThis is the arc-separation fact any counting proof of (S) needs. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.sameCycle_swap_mul_of_not_sameCycle","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.sameCycle_swap_mul_of_not_sameCycle","kind":"theorem","line":275,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrbitCount.lean#L275-L321","doc":"**(R)** Joining two different cycles with a transposition puts the two points in\none cycle of the product. **Proved.**\n\nIterating `τ = swap a b * σ` from `a` agrees with `σ` (because `τ x = σ x` whenever\n`σ x ∉ {a, b}`) all the way to the first return `σ^m a = a`, where the swap finally\nfires and sends `a` to `b`. Nothing on `a`'s orbit is `b`, or `a` and `b` would\nalready share a cycle; and nothing before step `m` is `a`, by minimality.\n\nNote it needs no `a ≠ b`: at `a = b` the hypothesis is already false, `SameCycle`\nbeing reflexive. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect_swap_mul_odd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect_swap_mul_odd","kind":"theorem","line":233,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrbitCount.lean#L233-L250","doc":"**Half of the ±1 step, free.** Multiplying by a transposition flips the defect's\nparity — so the change is odd, hence nonzero, and in particular `swap a b * σ ≠ σ`\nin defect. What this does NOT give is that the change is exactly one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.sign_eq_neg_one_pow_orbitDefect","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.sign_eq_neg_one_pow_orbitDefect","kind":"theorem","line":220,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrbitCount.lean#L220-L231","doc":"**The parity of the defect is the sign.** `sign σ = (−1)^{orbitDefect σ}`, since\n`sum + card ≡ sum − card (mod 2)` and Mathlib gives the sign as `(−1)^{sum + card}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.orbitCount_prod_of_disjoint_unit_defect","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.orbitCount_prod_of_disjoint_unit_defect","kind":"theorem","line":192,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrbitCount.lean#L192-L218","doc":"The orbit count of a disjoint product, in the form the free braid word has:\n`A` pairwise-disjoint blocks of defect one leave `card α − A` orbits.\n\nThis is the **free-word half of `ClassicalTraceFloor`'s hypotheses, provable now**:\nthe free word is a disjoint product of one identical block per nucleon, each block\nacting on that nucleon's own three strands with defect `1` (two orbits on three\npoints). With `card α = 3A` it gives `c(σ_free) = 3A − A = 2A`, which is the count\nthat file assumes.\n\nThe block's own defect enters as a hypothesis rather than being computed, exactly as\n`ClassicalTraceFloor` takes its counts — but it is now a statement about **one\nconcrete permutation of three points**, decidable in principle, instead of a claim\nabout the whole word at every `A`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect_prod_of_pairwise_disjoint","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect_prod_of_pairwise_disjoint","kind":"theorem","line":179,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrbitCount.lean#L179-L190","doc":"Additivity over a list of pairwise-disjoint permutations — the shape a braid word\nassembled one nucleon block at a time actually has. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect_mul_of_disjoint","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect_mul_of_disjoint","kind":"theorem","line":169,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrbitCount.lean#L169-L177","doc":"**The additive core.** Disjoint permutations have additive defect. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect_swap","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect_swap","kind":"theorem","line":162,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrbitCount.lean#L162-L167","doc":"A transposition has defect one: two support elements, one cycle. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect_le_card","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect_le_card","kind":"theorem","line":158,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrbitCount.lean#L158-L160","doc":"The defect never exceeds the support, so the orbit count never underflows. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.orbitCount_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.orbitCount_one","kind":"theorem","line":155,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrbitCount.lean#L155-L156","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect_one","kind":"theorem","line":152,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrbitCount.lean#L152-L153","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.orbitCount","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.orbitCount","kind":"def","line":149,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrbitCount.lean#L149-L150","doc":"The number of orbits of `σ` on `α`, fixed points included. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.orbitDefect","kind":"def","line":144,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrbitCount.lean#L144-L147","doc":"`orbitDefect σ = Σ_{c ∈ cycleType σ} (c − 1)`: the number of elements a\npermutation \"uses up\" beyond one representative per orbit. Additive over disjoint\npermutations, unlike the orbit count itself. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.card_cycleType_le_card_support","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.card_cycleType_le_card_support","kind":"theorem","line":134,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrbitCount.lean#L134-L142","doc":"Every cycle in `cycleType` has length at least two, so there are at most as many\ncycles as support elements — this is what makes the `ℕ` subtraction in `orbitDefect`\nan honest one. ","signatureChunk":0}]}